
<% content_for :title, "Mixed Symptoms Relapse Plan | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="relapse-plan-page relapse-plan-mixed-symptoms">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-hero">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <%= link_to "← ALL RELAPSE PLANS",
          toolbox_relapse_plans_path,
          class: "relapse-plan-back" %>

      <p class="relapse-plan-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / RELAPSE PLANS / MIXED SYMPTOMS
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>WIRED.</span>
        <span>MISERABLE.</span>
        <span>NOW WHAT?</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="relapse-plan-intro">
        YOUR SOBRIETY PLAN WHEN DEPRESSION
        AND MANIC SYMPTOMS COLLIDE.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / YOUR EXPERIENCE -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-yellow">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        01 / YOUR EXPERIENCE
      </p>

      <h2>
        HOW THIS<br>
        SHOWS UP.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-copy">

        <p>
          Mixed symptoms can feel like being pulled
          in opposite directions at the same time.
          You're miserable, irritable, or hopeless,
          but your thoughts won't slow down.
          Your body feels restless while
          your mind feels exhausted.
        </p>

        <p>
          You may have the despair of depression
          alongside the agitation, racing thoughts,
          reduced sleep, or impulsivity associated
          with mania. You don't necessarily feel
          happy or energized in a pleasant way.
          You can feel intensely uncomfortable
          and unable to settle.
        </p>

        <p>
          That combination can make immediate relief
          especially appealing. You want the noise
          to stop, your body to relax, or your
          thoughts to give you a break.
          Alcohol may seem like something
          that could accomplish all three.
        </p>

        <p>
          You might want to drink to slow your
          thoughts, escape depressive feelings,
          relieve agitation, or simply feel
          something other than what you're
          experiencing.
        </p>

        <p>
          But alcohol can further impair judgment,
          disrupt sleep, worsen mood symptoms,
          and reduce your ability to respond
          safely to impulses.
        </p>

        <p>
          Mixed symptoms also deserve particular
          attention because depressive hopelessness
          can occur alongside increased agitation
          and the energy to act impulsively.
          Since suicidal ideation can accompany
          your depressive episodes, don't dismiss
          those thoughts if they appear
          during a mixed state.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            You don't have to decide whether
            you're manic or depressed before
            recognizing that something
            needs attention.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / RECOGNIZING YOUR PATTERNS -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-black">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        02 / RECOGNIZING YOUR PATTERNS
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT ARE YOU<br>
        NOTICING?
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- MOOD & THOUGHTS -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>MOOD &amp; THOUGHTS</h3>

          <p>
            Pay attention when you're feeling
            depressed, hopeless, angry,
            or unusually irritable while
            your mind is racing.
          </p>

          <p>
            You may jump rapidly between
            thoughts, replay arguments,
            become overwhelmed by small
            problems, or feel unable
            to tolerate ordinary frustration.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your thinking can become absolute:
            "Nothing will get better,"
            "I can't stand this,"
            or "I need to do something
            right now."
          </p>

    
          <p>
            <strong>
              A thought can feel urgent,
              convincing, and unbearable
              without being something
              you have to act on.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- ENERGY & SLEEP -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>ENERGY &amp; SLEEP</h3>

          <p>
            You may feel physically exhausted
            but mentally overstimulated.
            Your body wants rest,
            yet your thoughts keep moving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for sleeping less than
            usual, difficulty falling asleep,
            waking frequently, or feeling
            driven to stay active despite
            being miserable.
          </p>

          <p>
            You might pace, become restless,
            start tasks without finishing
            them, or find it difficult
            to sit quietly.
          </p>

       

        </div>


        <!-- URGES & ACTIONS -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>URGES &amp; ACTIONS</h3>

          <p>
            Your patience may disappear,
            and the distance between
            an impulse and an action
            can become dangerously short.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for impulsive spending,
            angry messages, sudden decisions,
            reckless driving, or wanting
            to escape a situation
            immediately.
          </p>

          <p>
            You may feel desperate
            to change something,
            even when you don't
            know exactly what
            needs to change.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is a time to delay
            purchases, major commitments,
            arguments, and other
            high-consequence decisions.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you're experiencing
            unusually strong impulses,
            don't rely entirely on
            willpower. Involve someone
            you trust and create
            practical barriers
            before acting.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- ALCOHOL -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>WHAT YOU WANT ALCOHOL TO DO</h3>

          <p>
            You might want alcohol
            to slow your thoughts,
            reduce agitation,
            make depression feel
            less intense, or
            help you stop caring
            for a few hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            The argument may sound like:
            "I just need something
            to calm me down,"
            "I can't feel like
            this all night,"
            or "One drink would
            take the edge off."
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember that alcohol
            has provided temporary
            relief before. That's
            why the idea remains
            persuasive.
          </p>

          <p>
            But play the tape forward.
            Alcohol doesn't treat
            mixed mood symptoms,
            and intoxication can
            make impulsivity,
            emotional instability,
            and unsafe decisions
            harder to manage.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              You want relief
              from the symptoms.
              You don't need to
              add alcohol to them.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / YOUR IMMEDIATE PLAN -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-purple">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        03 / YOUR IMMEDIATE PLAN
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT HAPPENS<br>
        NEXT?
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- FIRST ACTIONS -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR FIRST ACTIONS</h3>

          <p>
            When you recognize a combination
            of depressive symptoms and
            manic agitation, don't wait
            for it to become unbearable
            before responding.
          </p>


          <p>
            Continue taking medication
            as prescribed, and follow
            your established care plan.
            Don't change dosages or
            add medications on your
            own to calm yourself down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reduce unnecessary demands.
            Put off major decisions,
            arguments, spending,
            and activities that require
            unusually good judgment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Focus on manageable basics:
            eat something, drink water,
            rest in a quiet environment,
            and keep your sleep
            schedule as consistent
            as possible.
          </p>

          
          <p>
            <strong>
              Mixed symptoms aren't
              something you have to
              solve through willpower.
              Early treatment matters.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- SURROUNDINGS -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR SURROUNDINGS</h3>

          <p>
            Make your immediate environment
            easier to manage. Reduce noise,
            unnecessary stimulation,
            arguments, and situations
            that increase agitation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid alcohol. Don't go to
            liquor stores, browse alcohol
            delivery, or place yourself
            somewhere drinking
            is readily available.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put your phone aside
            if you're tempted to
            send impulsive messages.
            Close shopping apps,
            postpone purchases,
            and ask a trusted
            person to help you
            maintain those limits.
          </p>

          


          <p>
            You don't need to
            eliminate every
            uncomfortable feeling.
            You need an environment
            that doesn't make
            an already difficult
            episode more dangerous.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / SUPPORT & SAFETY -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-pink">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        04 / SUPPORT &amp; SAFETY
      </p>

      <h2>
        HAVE THE<br>
        CONTACTS READY.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- PEOPLE & CLINICIANS -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR PEOPLE &amp; DOCTORS</h3>

          <p>
            Identify someone who can
            recognize when your mood
            or behavior is changing
            and will respond without
            encouraging impulsive
            decisions or drinking.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be direct about what
            you're experiencing.
            You can say:
            "I'm feeling depressed
            and agitated at the same
            time. My thoughts are
            racing, and I'm having
            trouble slowing down.
            I need some support."
          </p>

          <p>
            If you're thinking
            about drinking, tell
            someone before you
            make plans to obtain
            alcohol.
          </p>



          <p>
            <strong>
              Your support system
              needs accurate
              information to
              help you respond.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>


        

      </div>
<!-- BACK TO RELAPSE PLANS -->
<nav class="index-home-nav" aria-label="Return to relapse plans">
  <%= link_to "← BACK TO RELAPSE PLANS",
      toolbox_relapse_plans_path,
      class: "index-home-button" %>
</nav>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>
